"Kähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds (https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15970)\nciteturn4view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":345}); Total lines: 3025\nL323:  | $${\\mathcal{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}}):=({\\mathcal{C}}_{0}\\times{\\hat{M}}^{k})\\cap{\\mathcal{K}}({\\bar{r}})=$$  |\nL324:  | $$=\\Big\\{\\ (I,{\\mathbbm{x}})\\ \\big|\\ I\\in\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)\\cdot\\big({\\mathcal{C}}_{0}\\cap{\\it Cmpt}(M,\\omega)\\big),{\\mathbbm{x}}\\in{\\hat{M}}^{k}\\ \\textit{and}$$  |\nL325:  | $$\\Pi^{*}[\\omega]-\\pi\\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\\in H^{2}({\\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\\mathbbm{x}}};{\\mathbb{R}})\\ \\textit{is K\\\"{a}hler with respect to}\\ {\\widetilde{I}}\\ \\Big\\}.$$  |\nL326: Define the set ${\\tt{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}})\\subset{\\tt Teich}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\\omega)$ as\nL327:  | $${\\tt{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}}):={\\tt{K}}({\\bar{r}})\\cap{\\tt Teich}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M)={\\textit{pr}\\,}\\left({\\mathcal{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}})\\right)/\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)=$$  |\nL328:  | $$=\\Big\\{\\ \\iota\\in{\\tt Teich}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\\omega)\\ \\big|\\ \\exists I\\in{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}\\cap{\\it Cmpt}(M,\\omega),[I]=\\iota,\\ \\textit{and}\\ {\\mathbbm{x}}\\in{\\hat{M}}^{k}\\ \\textit{s.t.}$$  |\nL329:  | $$\\Pi^{*}[\\omega]-\\pi\\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\\in H^{2}({\\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\\mathbbm{x}}};{\\mathbb{R}})\\ \\textit{is K\\\"{a}hler with respect to}\\ {\\widetilde{I}}\\ \\Big\\}.$$  |\nL330: As we will see in cite24†5.6 , the fibers of ${\\textit{pr}\\,}:{\\mathcal{K}}({\\bar{r}})\\to\\mathit{CK}(M)$ are connected and therefore ${\\tt{K}}({\\bar{r}})$ (respectively, ${\\tt{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}})$) is connected if and only if so is ${\\mathcal{K}}({\\bar{r}})$ (respectively, ${\\mathcal{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}})$).\nL331: \nL332: Theorem 3.2\nL333: \nL334: Let $M$ be a closed manifold equipped with a Kähler-type symplectic form $\\omega$.\nL335: (I) The set $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})$ admits a Kähler-type embedding into $(M,\\omega)$ if and only if for some $I\\in{\\it Cmpt}(M,\\omega)$ and some ${\\mathbbm{x}}\\in{\\hat{M}}^{k}$ the cohomology class $\\Pi^{*}[\\omega]-\\pi\\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\\in H^{2}({\\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\\mathbbm{x}}};{\\mathbb{R}})$ is Kähler with respect to ${\\widetilde{I}}$.\nL336: More precisely, assume $I$ is a complex structure on $M$ compatible with $\\omega$ and $\\Sigma\\subset(M,I)$ is a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold. If $M={\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\\times{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}$ (with the product complex structure), we also allow $\\Sigma=({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\\times{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\\cup({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}\\times\\textrm{pt})$ (which is not smooth).\nL337: \nL338: Then the following conditions are equivalent:\nL339: \nL340:   * •\nL341: There exists ${\\mathbbm{x}}=(x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k})\\in{\\hat{M}}^{k}$, $x_{1},\\ldots,x_{k}\\in M\\setminus\\Sigma$, such that the cohomology class $\\Pi^{*}[\\omega]-\\pi\\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{2}e_{i}\\in H^{2}({\\widetilde{M}}_{I,{\\mathbbm{x}}};{\\mathbb{R}})$ is Kähler with respect to ${\\widetilde{I}}$.\nL342: \nL343:   * •\nL344: There exists an $[I]$-Kähler-type embedding $f:\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$ which is holomorphic with respect to the complex structure $\\phi_{1}^{*}I$ compatible with $\\omega$, where $\\{\\phi_{t}\\}_{0\\leq t\\leq 1}\\subset\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)$, $\\phi_{0}=Id$, is an isotopy such that $\\phi_{t}(\\Sigma)=\\Sigma$ for all $t\\in[0,1]$.\nL345: (II) Let ${\\mathcal{C}}_{0}$ be a connected component of $\\mathit{CK}(M)$. Assume that the set ${\\tt{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}})$ (or, equivalently, the set ${\\mathcal{K}}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}({\\bar{r}})$) is connected.\nL346: Then any two Kähler-type embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M,\\omega)$ favoring ${\\mathcal{C}}_{0}$ lie in the same $\\mathrm{Symp}(M,\\omega)\\cap\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)$-orbit. In particular, there exists $[I]\\in{\\tt Teich}_{{\\mathcal{C}}_{0}}(M,\\omega)$ such that both embeddings are of $[I]$-Kähler type.\nL347: If, in addition, $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M)$ acts transitively on the set of connected components of $\\mathit{CK}(M)$ compatible with $\\omega$, then any two Kähler-type embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M,\\omega)$ lie in the same $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}(M,\\omega)$-orbit.\nL348: \nL349: For the proof of cite12†3.1 see Section cite18†5 (cite20†5.2 ).\nL350: \nL351: Remark 3.3\nL352: \nL353: 1. For an analogue of part (I) of cite12†3.1 for tame embeddings see cite23†5.5 below.\nL354: 2. [cite51†Fl , Remark 5.2] states that the methods of [cite51†Fl ] can be used to prove a result that, in view of cite10†2.3 , is equivalent to Part (I) cite12†3.1 in the case where $\\omega$ is compatible with a complex structure $I$ on $M$, so that the complex manifold $(M,I)$ is projective and the cohomology class $[\\omega]\\in H^{2}(M;{\\mathbb{R}})$ is the first Chern class of an ample line bundle on $(M,I)$.\nL355: Recall that the symplectic blow-up construction (see e.g. [cite78†McDS , Sec. 7], cf. Section cite23†5.5 below) associates to a complex blow-up of a symplectic manifold at a point (with respect to, say, an almost complex structure compatible with the symplectic form and integrable near the point) a class of symplectic forms on the blow-up. We will call such a symplectic form on the blow-up a blow-up symplectic form.\nL356: A rational (4-dimensional) symplectic manifold is either ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}$ with a standard Fubini-Study symplectic form, or ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}\\times{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}$ with a product symplectic form, or their blow-up at several points equipped with a blow-up symplectic form.\nL357: A ruled (4-dimensional) symplectic manifold is a closed symplectic 4-manifold which is the total space of an ${\\mathbb{S}}^{2}$-fiber bundle over a closed connected orientable 2-dimensional surface, so that all the fibers are symplectic submanifolds [cite79†LalM ].\nL358: For rational or ruled closed symplectic manifolds of real dimension $4$ we have a stronger connectedness result, which follows directly from similar connectedness results for symplectic (not necessarily Kähler-type) embeddings of balls that were proved in [cite42†McD3 ] and [cite48†BLW ] (for previous partial results see [cite43†McD1 , cite44†McD2 , cite45†Lal , cite46†Bir1 ]).\nL359: \nL360: Theorem 3.4\nL361: Let $(M,\\omega)$ be a rational or ruled closed symplectic manifold, $\\dim_{\\mathbb{R}}M=4$. Let $\\Sigma\\subset(M,\\omega)$ be either of the following:\nL362: \nL363: (1) the empty set;\nL364: \nL365: (2) a finite union of closed compact symplectic submanifolds (without boundary) of real dimension 2 whose pairwise intersections (if they exist) are transverse and $\\omega$–orthogonal;\nL366: \nL367: (3) a Lagrangian submanifold which is diffeomorphic to either ${\\mathbb{S}}^{2}$ or ${\\mathbb{R}}P^{2}$.\nL368: Then for any $k\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$ and any $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}>0$, the following claims hold:\nL369: \nL370: (I) Any two Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\\to(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$ lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$-action. In particular, (for fixed $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}$) the space of all such Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings is connected.\nL371: (II) If there exists an $[I]$-Kähler-type, respectively tame, embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\\to(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$, then any symplectic embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\\to(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$ is of $[I]$-Kähler type, respectively tame.\nL372: \nL373: Proof of cite12†3.1 :\nL374: \nL375: Any two symplectic embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$ lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$-action:\nL376: - In case (1) this was proved in [cite42†McD3 , Cor. 1.5].\nL377: \nL378: - In case (2), where $\\Sigma\\subset(M,\\omega)$ is just a symplectic submanifold, this was essentially proved in [cite42†McD3 , Cor. 1.5] – see [cite48†BLW , Prop. 2.1] where more details of the proof are spelled out in the case where the symplectic submanifold $\\Sigma\\subset(M,\\omega)$ is a sphere; in fact [cite80†McDO , Cor. 1.2.13], used in the proof of [cite48†BLW , Prop. 2.1], yields the same claim for any $\\Sigma$ as in (2).\nL379: - In case (3), this is proved in [cite48†BLW , Thm. 1.1].\nL380: \nL381: Since $[I]$-Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings into $(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$ are mapped by the $\\mathrm{Symp}^{c}_{0}(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega)$-action into $[I]$-Kähler-type, respectively tame, embeddings, we get the results of both parts of the theorem.\nL382: \nL383: Now let us discuss applications of cite12†3.1 and cite12†3.1 to particular symplectic manifolds.\nL384: ### 3.2 The case of projective spaces and their products\nL385: \nL386: Denote by $\\omega_{FS}$, or by $\\omega_{FS,n}$, the Fubini-Study form on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ normalized so that $\\int_{{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}}\\omega_{FS}=\\pi$. Let $I_{st}$ be the standard complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$.\nL387: \nL388: We also set ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{0}:=\\textrm{pt}$.\nL389: \nL390: Theorem 3.5\nL391: Consider the manifold $M:={\\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\\times\\ldots\\times{\\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{m}}$, $n_{1},\\ldots,n_{m}>0$, $n_{1}+\\ldots+n_{m}=:n$, endowed with the symplectic form $\\omega_{\\mathbbm{c}}:=c_{1}\\omega_{FS,n_{1}}\\oplus\\ldots\\oplus c_{m}\\omega_{FS,n_{m}}$, ${\\mathbbm{c}}:=(c_{1},\\ldots,c_{m})\\in({\\mathbb{R}}_{>0})^{m}$. Let $I$ be the complex structure on $M$ which is the product of the standard complex structures on the factors.\nL392: Let $l_{1},\\ldots,l_{m}\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$ so that $[l_{1}:\\ldots:l_{m}]=[c_{1}:\\ldots:c_{m}]\\in{\\mathbb{R}}P^{m}$. Let\nL393: \nL394:  | $$k:=\\frac{(n_{1}+\\ldots+n_{m})!}{n_{1}!\\cdot\\ldots\\cdot n_{m}!}l_{1}^{n_{1}}\\cdot\\ldots\\cdot l_{m}^{n_{m}}.$$  |\nL395: \nL396: Let $\\Sigma\\subset(M,I)$ be a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold. If $M={\\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\\times{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}$, we also allow $\\Sigma=({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}-1}\\times{\\mathbb{C}}P^{1})\\cup({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n_{1}}\\times\\textrm{pt})$.\nL397: Then Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of $k$ equal balls into $(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{\\mathbbm{c}})$ are unobstructed.\nL398: More precisely, if $k{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega)$, then for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold $\\Sigma\\subset(M,I)$, there exists an $[I]$-Kähler-type embedding of $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r)$ into $(M,\\omega)$ which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on $M$ that is compatible with $\\omega$ and isotopic to $I$ by an isotopy preserving $\\Sigma$ (as a set).\nL399: \nL400: For the proof of cite13†3.2 see Section cite25†6 (cite25†6 ).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nKähler-type embeddings of balls into symplectic manifolds (https://arxiv.org/html/2211.15970)\nciteturn4view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"lineno\":422}); Total lines: 3025\nL397: Then Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of $k$ equal balls into $(M\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{\\mathbbm{c}})$ are unobstructed.\nL398: More precisely, if $k{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega)$, then for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold $\\Sigma\\subset(M,I)$, there exists an $[I]$-Kähler-type embedding of $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r)$ into $(M,\\omega)$ which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on $M$ that is compatible with $\\omega$ and isotopic to $I$ by an isotopy preserving $\\Sigma$ (as a set).\nL399: \nL400: For the proof of cite13†3.2 see Section cite25†6 (cite25†6 ).\nL401: For $n_{1}=\\ldots=n_{m}=1$, $l_{1}=\\ldots=l_{m}=l$, $c_{1}=\\ldots=c_{m}=1$ and $\\Sigma=\\emptyset$, cite13†3.2 yields the following corollary.\nL402: \nL403: Corollary 3.6\nL404: \nL405: Let $M:=({\\mathbb{C}}P^{1})^{m}$. Consider a symplectic form $\\omega$ on $M$ which is the product of equal Fubini-Study forms on the ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}$-factors of $M$. Let $I$ be the complex structure on $M$ which is the product of the standard complex structures on the ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{1}$-factors of $M$.\nL406: Then for any $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$, Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of $m!l^{m}$ equal balls into $(M,\\omega)$ are unobstructed.\nL407: \nL408: More precisely, if $m!l^{m}{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(M,\\omega)$, then there exists a Kähler-type embedding of $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{m!l^{m}}B^{2n}(r)$ into $(M,\\omega)$ which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on $M$ that is compatible with $\\omega$ and isotopic to $I$.\nL409: Now let us consider the case of a single complex projective space.\nL410: \nL411: Theorem 3.7\nL412: \nL413: A. For each $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$, Kähler-type embeddings of disjoint unions of $l^{n}$ equal balls into $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$ are unobstructed.\nL414: More precisely, if $l^{n}{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$, then for any complex structure $J$ on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$ (and, in particular, for the standard complex structure $I_{st}$) and for each proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold $\\Sigma\\subset({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},J)$, there exists a $[J]$-Kähler-type embedding of $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)$ into $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$ which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ that is compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$ and isotopic to $J$ by an isotopy preserving $\\Sigma$ (as a set).\nL415: B. The group $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$ acts transitively on the set of connected components of $\\mathit{CK}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n})$ compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$.\nL416: \nL417: C. For any $k\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$ and $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}>0$, any two Kähler-type embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$ (if they exist!) lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}_{H}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$-action.\nL418: They lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})\\cap\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n})$-action if and only if they favor a common connected component of $\\mathit{CK}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n})$. In the latter case there exists $[I]\\in{\\tt Teich}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$ such that both embeddings are of $[I]$-Kähler type.\nL419: \nL420: Part A of cite13†3.2 follows from cite13†3.2 . For the proof of parts B and C of cite13†3.2 see Section cite25†6 (cite25†6 ).\nL421: \nL422: Remark 3.8\nL423: Let us say that a Kähler-type complex structure $I$ on a closed manifold $M$ is rigid, if the connected component of $\\mathit{CK}(M)$ containing $I$ coincides with the $\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)$-orbit of $I$ (or, in other words, if any Kähler-type complex structure on $M$ obtained from $I$ by a smooth deformation is, in fact, isotopic to $I$).\nL424: As we will see in cite25†6 , any Kähler-type complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ is rigid. More examples of rigid complex structures include so-called ${\\mathcal{Z}}$-manifolds (i.e., Calabi-Yau manifolds with $h^{n-1,1}=0$, [cite81†CDP ]).\nL425: The proof of part C of cite13†3.2 allows to generalize the last claim of part C as follows: Assume $(M,\\omega)$ is a closed manifold such that $\\omega$ is compatible with a rigid complex structure $I$. Let ${\\mathcal{C}}_{0}$ be a connected component of $\\mathit{CK}(M)$ containing $I$. Then two Kähler-type embeddings $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{2n}(r_{i})\\to(M,\\omega)$ favoring ${\\mathcal{C}}_{0}$ lie in the same orbit of the $\\mathrm{Symp}(M,\\omega)\\cap\\mathrm{Diff}_{0}(M)$-action.\nL426: Applying part A of cite13†3.2 with $\\Sigma=\\emptyset$, with $I_{st}=J$ being fixed, and with $\\omega_{FS}$ being varied by an isotopy, we get the following immediate corollary.\nL427: \nL428: Corollary 3.9\nL429: \nL430: Let $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$.\nL431: Then for any $r>0$ such that $l^{n}{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},\\omega_{FS})$ there exists a Kähler form $\\omega$ on $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})$ isotopic to $\\omega_{FS}$ and such that the Kähler manifold $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st},\\omega)$ admits a Kähler (i.e., both holomorphic and symplectic) embedding of $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)$ with the standard flat Kähler metric on it.\nL432: In particular, part A of cite13†3.2 and cite13†3.2 mean that by changing either $I_{st}$ or $\\omega_{FS}$ by an isotopy one can get a Kähler metric on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ which is the standard flat Kähler metric on $l^{n}$ disjoint balls in ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{n}$ that fill out an arbitrarily large portion of the volume of the manifold.\nL433: \nL434: Remark 3.10\nL435: \nL436: For $l=1$ and $n=2$ cite13†3.2 was proved in [cite50†Ec ].\nL437: Applying cite13†3.2 with $J=I_{st}$ and with the hyperplane $\\Sigma={\\mathbb{C}}P^{n-1}\\subset({\\mathbb{C}}P^{n},I_{st})$, we get the following corollary.\nL438: \nL439: Corollary 3.11\nL440: For any $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$ and any $r>0$ such that $l^{n}{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{2n}(1),\\omega_{0})$ there exists a Kähler-type embedding of $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{l^{n}}B^{2n}(r)$ into $(B^{2n}(1),\\omega_{0})$ which is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on $B^{2n}(1)$ isotopic to $J_{0}$ and compatible with $\\omega_{0}$.\nL441: \nL442: Now let us focus on the case of $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\\omega_{FS})$.\nL443: \nL444: Proposition 3.12\nL445: There is only one connected component of $\\mathit{CK}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2})$ compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$ – it is the connected component of $\\mathit{CK}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2})$ containing $I_{st}$. Any two complex structures in that connected component are isotopic.\nL446: \nL447: For the proof of cite13†3.2 see Section cite25†6 (cite25†6 ). We do not know whether $\\mathit{CK}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2})$ has more than one connected component – see cite25†6 .\nL448: \nL449: cite13†3.2 yields the following immediate corollary.\nL450: \nL451: Corollary 3.13\nL452: Any Kähler-type embedding (of any disjoint union of domains) into $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\\omega_{FS})$ is of $[I_{st}]$-Kähler type: it is symplectic with respect to $\\omega_{FS}$ and holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}$ isotopic to $I_{st}$ and compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$.\nL453: \nL454: Recall the following theorem of McDuff-Polterovich [cite57†McDP ].\nL455: \nL456: Theorem 3.14\nL457: \nL458: Let $\\Sigma\\subset({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})$ be a proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold.\nL459: A. Assume that $1\\leq k\\leq 8$ and $r_{1}\\geq r_{2}\\geq\\ldots\\geq r_{k}>0$.\nL460: \nL461: Then the following conditions are equivalent:\nL462: \nL463:   * •\nL464: \nL465: There exists a symplectic embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$.\nL466: \nL467:   * •\nL468: \nL469: The radii $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}$ satisfy the following inequalities (listed in [cite57†McDP , Cor. 1.3G]):\nL470: \nL471: (v) $\\sum_{i=1}^{k}r_{i}^{4}<1$ (volume inequality),\nL472: \nL473: (c1) $r_{1}^{2}+r_{2}^{2}<1$, if $2\\leq k\\leq 8$,\nL474: (c2) $r_{1}^{2}+\\ldots+r_{5}^{2}<2$, if $5\\leq k\\leq 8$,\nL475: \nL476: (c3) $2r_{1}^{2}+\\sum_{i=2}^{7}r_{i}^{2}<3$, if $7\\leq k\\leq 8$,\nL477: \nL478: (c4) $2r_{1}^{2}+2r_{2}^{2}+2r_{3}^{2}+r_{4}^{2}+\\ldots+r_{8}^{2}<4$, if $k=8$,\nL479: \nL480: (c5) $2\\sum_{i=1}^{6}r_{i}^{2}+r_{7}^{2}+r_{8}^{2}<5$, if $k=8$,\nL481: \nL482: (c6) $3r_{1}^{2}+2\\sum_{i=2}^{8}r_{i}^{2}<6$, if $k=8$.\nL483: B. Symplectic embeddings of a disjoint union of $k=l^{2}$, $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$, equal balls into $({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$ are unobstructed: if $k{\\rm{Vol}\\,}(B^{4}(r),\\omega_{0})<{\\rm{Vol}\\,}({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},\\omega_{FS})$, then there exists a symplectic embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$.\nL484: \nL485: Here is an extension of cite13†3.2 to Kähler-type embeddings of balls into ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}$.\nL486: \nL487: Theorem 3.15\nL488: A. Assume that $1\\leq k\\leq 8$ and $r_{1}\\geq r_{2}\\geq\\ldots\\geq r_{k}>0$.\nL489: \nL490: Then for any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold $\\Sigma\\subset({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})$, any symplectic embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$ is, in fact, of $[I_{st}]$-Kähler type: it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}$ that is compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$ and isotopic to $I_{st}$ by an isotopy fixing $\\Sigma$ (as a set).\nL491: Accordingly, by part A of cite13†3.2 , such a Kähler-type embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r_{i})\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$ exists if and only if the radii $r_{1},\\ldots,r_{k}$ satisfy the inequalities (v), (c1)-(c6) listed in part A of cite13†3.2 .\nL492: B. For any proper (possibly empty) complex submanifold $\\Sigma\\subset({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2},I_{st})$ and any $k=l^{2}$, $l\\in{\\mathbb{Z}}_{>0}$, any symplectic embedding $\\bigsqcup_{i=1}^{k}B^{4}(r)\\to({\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}\\setminus\\Sigma,\\omega_{FS})$ is, in fact, of $[I_{st}]$-Kähler type: it is holomorphic with respect to a complex structure on ${\\mathbb{C}}P^{2}$ that is compatible with $\\omega_{FS}$ and isotopic to $I_{st}$ by an isotopy preserving $\\Sigma$ (as a set).\n"
